The chief looked at his empty hands. "How can I? You've already rewritten what happened. In your telling, I'm the villain. In mine, you are."

"Then we're trapped," she replied.

"Yes."

Both understood now: It didn't matter who was right. It didn't matter what actually happened. They each carried a truth so certain, so incompatible, that no bridge could span the gap.

The chief kept his five goods. The elder kept her five. Neither gained the multiplication that cooperation would have brought.

They'd each chosen certainty over possibility—the certainty of their own narrative over the possibility of reconciliation.

And so the valley grew two separate histories, two separate truths, and no shared future at all.

The rivers never touched again.

