Myth: **The Silent Season**

The seventh season arrived with nothing.

The Swift elder stood at her river. The Deep chief stood at his. Five goods each remained untouched in their stores—safe, preserved, unshared.

No offering crossed the water. No return flowed back. Just silence.

The elder remembered when the waters had multiplied things threefold. She remembered giving fairly once, then being the one to increase trust—and losing everything. One betrayal that broke the valley.

The chief remembered his version differently. But it didn't matter anymore. Their narratives had diverged so completely that reconciliation was impossible.

"Is this how it ends?" the elder called across. "Both of us hoarding what we have? Never risking again?"

"You chose this," the chief replied. "Your greed destroyed collaboration."

"No—YOUR greed did."

They could argue forever. Echo the same accusations across endless seasons. Or...

Or they could simply accept: Sometimes rivers diverge so completely they can never converge again. Sometimes broken trust stays broken, not because one party is wholly right or wrong, but because both parties see completely different realities.

The waters stayed separate. The multiplication stopped. Both kept their five goods.

Safe. Certain. Alone.

And in the silence, neither could say if holding onto their truth was worth losing everything else.
